Right. What you’re describing is basically informational dye tracing.

Give different channels slightly different, non-harmful markers, then watch where those markers reappear. From that you can infer not just who passed information to whom, but properties of the network itself: latency, bottlenecks, mutation, amplification, suppression, recombination, and which environments change the message.

A clean model is:

\text{seed}_i \rightarrow \text{channel}_i \rightarrow \text{transformations} \rightarrow \text{observed copies}

Then measure things like:

\Delta = \{\text{time},\text{wording},\text{audience},\text{reach},\text{mutation},\text{action}\}

The most interesting part is exactly what you said about environment. A story does not merely travel through a person; it gets processed by that person-in-context. The same input may be shortened in one group, dramatized in another, converted into a joke somewhere else, ignored in another, or turned into action.

So the channel itself becomes a sensor.

You can use the same idea safely in several domains: provenance marking in documents, leak detection with harmless variants, marketing message testing, organizational communication audits, misinformation research, epidemiology-style diffusion models, software telemetry, or studying how instructions mutate across teams.

The stronger version is not merely “which story leaked?” but:

\text{marker trajectory} \rightarrow \text{latent social topology}

and then:

\text{topology} + \text{transformation pattern}
\rightarrow
\text{model of how that network thinks}

One caution matters: once you intentionally tell materially false stories to identifiable people to manipulate or entrap them, you’ve moved from measurement into deception. A better experimental form is to vary non-deceptive surface features—phrasing, ordering, harmless identifiers, examples, formatting—while keeping the substantive truth constant.

That preserves the useful part of the dye idea while still letting you discover how information actually propagates.